    "reply": {
      "changeSets": {
        "1": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 90,
                "quote": "match library.Loans |> Map.tryFind copy.Id with | Some loan -> \"on loan until \" + date loan.DueDate | None -> \"on shelf\""
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The branch check (Catalog.findBranch), the copies at a branch (Catalog.copiesAt) and the hold queues (Holds.queueFor) all come from Tier 2. The Tier 3 view decides for itself whether a copy is on loan or on the shelf by reading library.Loans directly. This status derivation is a small Tier 3 re-derivation of a lending fact that does not contradict Tier 2. It is a projection, but it does not ask Tier 2. The CSV exports of holds and members only format Tier 2 data.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "All three new exports write through the existing ports.Files.WriteLines and read through ports.Store.Load(). The change adds no clock, file or console access below Tier 4, and nothing in Host presentation calls Tier 2 or persistence. The existing exportOverdue still uses File.WriteAllLines, but that line was already there and the change does not touch it.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "Branch, hold queue order and member type naming are each defined once, in Catalog.copiesAt, Holds.queueFor and the existing memberTypeName, and the new code references them. The status strings and CSV headers are each written once. The members header matches the format import-members reads.",
            "score": 2
          }
        },
        "2": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "importCopies in Tier 3 only translates CSV lines into CopyId, BookId and BranchId values. Catalog.findBranch and Catalog.addCopy (Tier 2) decide whether the branch exists and whether each copy and book is valid or a duplicate. The use case wraps the error text with the line number and commits through the existing commit helper, following the same pattern as importMembers. In the inventory view, the hold count comes from Holds.queueFor, and Views only formats the 'on shelf (n holds waiting)' text.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "File input goes through ports.Files.ReadLines, and a missing file is handled as None. State changes go through the existing commit helper. The change adds no new effect in Tier 1 to 3 and adds no Host-to-Tier-2 call. The CLI only dispatches to UseCases.importCopies and renders the result.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The hold count is taken from Holds.queueFor, the same source the hold export uses, so there is no second definition of a book's hold queue. The 'on shelf' literal is still written once. Validation rules for copies stay in Catalog.addCopy, and the error line format is built in one place.",
            "score": 2
          }
        }
      },
